  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-80182",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the diagram, we have $f(1)=1$ (we have an arrow from $1\\in X$ to $1\\in Y$). So, there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, for all $y$ contained within $Y,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
